  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find the **exact** solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/assets/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the fourth quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$",
